What is Sensor Calibration?

Sensor calibration is the process of comparing a sensor's output with a known reference value and adjusting its readings so they accurately represent the real-world quantity being measured.

Calibration helps remove errors caused by manufacturing variations, aging, environmental conditions, and installation differences.

Example

Suppose a temperature sensor should read 25°C, but it displays 27°C. After calibration, the sensor is adjusted so that it reports the correct temperature.
Why is Sensor Calibration Important in IoT Systems?

IoT systems rely on sensors to make decisions automatically. If sensor readings are inaccurate, the entire system may behave incorrectly.

Importance of Calibration
Improves Accuracy
Ensures the sensor measures values close to the actual physical quantity.
Increases Reliability
Devices provide consistent readings over time.
Reduces Measurement Errors
Corrects offset and scaling errors.
Supports Better Automation
Accurate data leads to better control decisions in IoT applications.
Saves Resources
Prevents unnecessary actions, such as excessive irrigation or cooling, reducing water and energy usage.
Two-Point Calibration Method

The two-point calibration method uses two known reference conditions to establish the measurement range:

Dry Point (0% Moisture)
Wet Point (100% Moisture)

These two values are then used to convert any intermediate sensor reading into a moisture percentage.


