I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit) is a two-wire serial communication protocol used to connect a microcontroller (such as Arduino or ESP32) with multiple IoT sensors and devices. It allows several devices to communicate over the same bus using unique addresses.

SDA (Serial Data): Carries the data between the master (microcontroller) and slave (sensor).
SCL (Serial Clock): Carries the clock signal generated by the master to synchronize communication.

I2C Addressing:

Every I2C device has a unique 7-bit or 10-bit address.
The master sends the address of the desired sensor first.
Only the sensor with the matching address responds, while all others ignore the communication.
This allows multiple sensors to share the same SDA and SCL lines.

Examples of common IoT sensors using I2C:

BMP280 – Temperature and Pressure Sensor
MPU6050 – Accelerometer and Gyroscope
BME280 – Temperature, Humidity, and Pressure Sensor

Key Advantage: I2C reduces wiring by allowing multiple sensors to communicate using only two communication wires (SDA and SCL) along with power and ground.